WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:06.210
Where does the campy 1966 "Batman" movie fit
in among the grim and gritty "Dark Knight"

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.090
films of the 21st century?

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:11.620
Keep watching, because the answer awaits!

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:16.430
You could make a case that 2005's "Batman
Begins" is a more practical starting point

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:21.400
for a neophyte than 1989's smash hit "Batman"
by director Tim Burton.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.779
There's a solid basis for that argument, and
it has to do with character motivation.

00:00:25.779 --> 00:00:31.470
In 1989's "Batman," eventual Joker Jack Napier
murders young Bruce Wayne's parents in front

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.470
of him.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:37.300
Traditional versions of Batman's origin depict
nervous, incompetent, anonymous mugger Joe

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.120
Chill gunning down poor Thomas and Martha
Wayne.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.949
That might sound like a trivial change, but
it actually makes a significant difference:

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:50.420
By removing the randomness and mundanity from
Batman's foundational violence and attributing

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:56.129
it to a major supervillain, Burton makes Batman's
vendetta about a specific evil dude rather

00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:59.370
than the abstract concept of crime.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.489
So why is 1989's "Batman" the place to start?

00:01:03.489 --> 00:01:09.340
It's simple: this version set the standard
for the plethora of superhero blockbusters

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.340
to follow.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:15.370
Between its record-breaking box office returns
and the corresponding merchandising bonanza

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:20.120
that followed, "Batman" was everywhere during
that final year of the '80s.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:25.420
We are still feeling the ripples its enormous
splash left in the cinematic marketplace.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:31.320
Thus, it makes sense to watch this "Batman"
before "Batman Begins," because without the

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.330
former, the latter would have never been made.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:40.850
If you start with 1989's "Batman," then your
best bet for sustained continuity is the path

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.920
laid out by the '90s Batman movie timeline.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.100
That makes your second stop Tim Burton's follow-up:
1992's "Batman Returns."

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.910
This sequel marks Michael Keaton's second
outing as the Caped Crusader and also boasts

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:59.560
Michelle Pfeiffer and Danny DeVito delivering
quintessential performances as Catwoman and

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.160
the Penguin, respectively.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:07.340
With its dark fairy tale aesthetic and emphasis
on damaged outcasts, "Batman Returns" probably

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:13.140
has more in common with other Tim Burton projects
like 1990's "Edward Scissorhands" than most

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.780
other superhero movies.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:19.600
It's certainly too macabre for small children,
which is why there was such a huge backlash

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:25.470
after Warner Bros. deployed misleadingly kid-friendly
marketing, including McDonald's Happy Meals toys.

00:02:26.542 --> 00:02:31.140
But as long as you keep the kiddies away from
this flick, you'll have a great time.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.190
You will, however, notice a major departure
in tone once you move on.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:40.091
Burton and Keaton both left The Caped Crusader
behind following "Batman Returns," and the

00:02:40.091 --> 00:02:44.720
decision-makers at Warner Bros., rattled by
the backlash, decided Gotham City needed to

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.870
get a lot less gothic.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:53.810
A more accurate title for 1995's "Batman Forever"
might be "The Bright Knight Returns."

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:57.640
Fans have been describing Joel Schumacher's
[SHOO-mah-kur] pair of Batman movies as torturous

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.000
viewing experiences for years.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.290
They're certainly a lot more colorful and
less serious than either Burton movie — and,

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.210
as a certain cultural critic once asked,

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.770
"Why so serious?"

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:17.150
1995's "Batman Forever" was a commercially
successful film, though reviews were mediocre at best.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:25.330
It is also utterly consumed by the all-encompassing
black hole of kinetic absurdity that is mid-'90s Jim Carrey.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:32.569
Fresh off mega-star turns in 1994's "Ace Ventura:
Pet Detective'' and "The Mask," Carrey's relentless,

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:38.360
rubber-faced Riddler devours this movie, much
to the chagrin of co-star Tommy Lee Jones.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.540
Carrey told Norm MacDonald that Jones was
very upfront about his disdain for him.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:47.770
"He went like to hug me, and he said, 'I hate
you!'

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.960
And Jones wasn't the only cast member having
a rough time.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.180
Effectively immobilized by his heavy Batsuit,
Val Kilmer left the Batman franchise after

00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:00.810
"Batman Forever" in order to work on movies
where he got to act instead of just fill a

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:01.810
rubber costume.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:07.690
Still, "Batman Forever" is entertaining, and
an important step in the Dark Knight's cinematic evolution.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:11.620
Notably, it marks the first time Bruce Wayne's
introduction to Dick Grayson is captured on

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.920
the silver screen.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.709
While the movies are more expensive and, in
theory, designed for a broader audience, the

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:25.370
most influential version of Batman from the
'90s is undoubtedly "Batman: The Animated Series."

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:30.979
This 1992 cartoon takes its tonal cue from
the Burton films while presenting a thoughtful,

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:35.139
emotionally mature, kid-friendly iteration
of Gotham City.

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:39.819
So why did Warner Bros. think they had to
make Batman a grinning goofball to appeal

00:04:39.819 --> 00:04:44.479
to children when their own TV show, based
on the same intellectual property, made a

00:04:44.479 --> 00:04:46.580
strong case to the contrary?

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:47.949
Who knows.

00:04:47.949 --> 00:04:51.529
But we live with the result: 1997's "Batman
and Robin."

00:04:51.529 --> 00:04:56.819
"Batman and Robin" combines the intellectual
development of "Mighty Morphin Power Rangers"

00:04:56.819 --> 00:05:01.599
with the camp of the 1960s "Batman" TV show,
but - not as good as either.

00:05:01.599 --> 00:05:06.360
Basically, the plot entails George Clooney's
Batman almost breaking up with Robin because

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.199
Dick Grayson is visibly pushing 30 and makes
no sense as a "Boy Wonder."

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:15.659
Meanwhile, Arnold Schwarzenegger, occupying
the villain spot as Mr. Freeze alongside Uma

00:05:15.659 --> 00:05:21.610
Thurman's Poison Ivy, deploys a deadly array
of absolutely terrible ice puns.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:23.520
It is groan-worthy.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.430
It is tiresome.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:29.520
But if you get some friends together who are
down to laugh at a low point in Batman's history,

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.789
it can be pretty fun to hate-watch.

00:05:32.789 --> 00:05:37.309
After "Batman Forever" and "Batman and Robin"
have conditioned you into processing a campy

00:05:37.309 --> 00:05:41.669
Caped Crusader, you should be more receptive
to the wholesome hijinks of Adam West and

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:45.210
Burt Ward in 1966's "Batman: The Movie."

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:50.580
Essentially a feature-length episode of the
1960s television series, this flick is defined

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:58.337
by a massive villain team-up: The Joker, the
Penguin, Catwoman, and the Riddler are all present.

00:05:58.337 --> 00:06:03.240
"Batman: The Movie" also includes Shark Repellent
Bat Spray, a dehydration device that reduces

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.839
its victims to powder, and a sequence in which
our hero declares,

00:06:06.839 --> 00:06:11.020
"Some days, you just can't get rid of a bomb!"

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:15.629
While there are certain rules Batman always
has to follow to remain a coherent character,

00:06:15.629 --> 00:06:21.139
adhering to grim humorlessness and brutality
are not necessarily among them.

00:06:21.139 --> 00:06:26.449
If the Joel Schumacher films are bad, it's
because they rely too heavily on commercialization

00:06:26.449 --> 00:06:30.289
— not because they let Batman smile every
once in a while.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:36.066
The mid-'60s live-action Batman endures as
a positive example of a family-friendly Bright Knight.

00:06:36.847 --> 00:06:42.199
Galvanized by a need to course-correct for
the empty-headed neon flamboyance of "Batman

00:06:42.199 --> 00:06:47.499
and Robin," Warner Bros. tapped Christopher
Nolan — then known for directing psychological

00:06:47.499 --> 00:06:51.740
thrillers like 2000's "Memento" and 2002's
"Insomnia."

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:58.529
With Nolan at the helm, they would pull Batman
back to his gritty, street-level roots.

00:06:58.529 --> 00:07:04.259
2005's "Batman Begins" marks the start of
a new timeline: Events that unfold in the

00:07:04.259 --> 00:07:08.650
Burton and Schumacher movies never occurred
in this iteration of Gotham City.

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:14.330
Christian Bale's Bruce Wayne transforms from
self-destructive rich kid to ninja trainee

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:19.439
to terrifying urban protector with nary a
Bat-credit card in sight.

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:25.279
The performatively immortal Raʼs al Ghul
doles out his master plan more like a megalomaniacal

00:07:25.279 --> 00:07:29.080
Bond villain than any literal or figurative
clown.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:34.300
The closest thing to camp comes in the form
of Scarecrow: a living haunted house prop

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.419
with a vicious streak.

00:07:36.419 --> 00:07:39.610
Scarecrow steals his share of scenes in "Batman
Begins."

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:44.409
He could've been the breakout Gotham villain
of his generation had it not been for the

00:07:44.409 --> 00:07:52.020
arrival of an even more deranged update of
a Batman enemy in the follow-up film.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:58.419
If you're going to watch just one Batman film,
it should be 2008's "The Dark Knight."

00:07:58.419 --> 00:08:03.909
Not only is this thematically rich sequel
to "Batman Begins" widely considered the greatest

00:08:03.909 --> 00:08:09.430
Batman movie, but many believe it's the best
superhero movie of all time.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:14.249
Christopher Nolan — along with brother Jonathan
Nolan and prolific screenwriter David S. Goyer

00:08:14.249 --> 00:08:18.939
— delivers a psychological action thriller
for the ages.

00:08:18.939 --> 00:08:24.849
Heath Ledger set the precedent by which live-action
supervillains continue to be judged.

00:08:24.849 --> 00:08:30.610
Christian Bale, Maggie Gyllenhaal, Aaron Eckhart,
and Gary Oldman crank out stellar performances

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:35.030
as well, though they're all instantly overshadowed.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:39.110
Ledger's Joker is simply too spectacular to
be ignored.

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:42.979
"You want to know how I got these scars?"

00:08:42.979 --> 00:08:47.230
Tragically, and famously, Ledger passed away
at the age of 28 before "The Dark Knight"

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:48.230
was released.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:52.740
He went on to win the Academy Award for Best
Actor in a Supporting Role for his work as

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:54.520
The Joker.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:09:00.870
In order to examine 2012's "The Dark Knight
Rises" for what it truly is, we have to push

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.190
a mountain of critical discourse aside.

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:07.500
Every possible opinion about "The Dark Knight
Rises" has been debated at length.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:09.470
Is it the best Batman movie ever?

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.540
Is it the worst Batman movie ever?

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:13.260
Is it in between?

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:18.259
The fact is, anything following "The Dark
Knight" was always going to feel like a tiny

00:09:18.259 --> 00:09:19.580
bit of a let-down.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:23.880
If you accept that, however, you'll realize
this film has a lot to offer.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:28.290
Bale is as strong a Batman as ever, going
out on a definite high note.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:33.350
As for the villains, Anne Hathaway adds a
world-weary sense of snark to Catwoman's classic

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:35.890
moral ambiguity that works like gangbusters.

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:37.779
And then there's Bane.

00:09:37.779 --> 00:09:43.000
Tom Hardy's genuine presence as the masked
baddie is irresistible, even if you can't

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:45.860
always understand what he's saying.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:50.314
Generally speaking, most people agree that
Joel Schumacher's version of Batman wasn't very good.

00:09:50.314 --> 00:09:55.120
Most people also agree that Christopher Nolan's
version of Batman was very good.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:01.360
But there's no consensus on the most polarizing
Batman of all, as tons of fans both love and

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.019
hate Zack Snyder's take on the Dark Knight.

00:10:04.019 --> 00:10:10.160
Whatever your personal take on Batfleck happens
to be, it's safe to say 2016's "Batman v Superman:

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:15.350
Dawn of Justice" is where the Caped Crusader
gets real weird.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:20.410
After observing that Superman can level an
entire major city whenever he feels like it,

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.209
Batman devises a plan to put the Man of Steel
in the ground.

00:10:23.209 --> 00:10:27.470
Or maybe Lex Luthor manipulates Batman into
going after Superman?

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:33.459
We're still not entirely clear on the plot
of "Batman v Superman," which received infamously

00:10:33.459 --> 00:10:35.120
terrible reviews.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:39.899
What is clear is that Ben Affleck is doing
his best to play a Batman who has lived long

00:10:39.899 --> 00:10:42.779
enough to become the villain.

00:10:42.779 --> 00:10:48.240
If your purpose in this endeavor is to watch
every Batman movie, then the 2017 edit of

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.420
"Justice League," overseen by director Joss
Whedon, and 2021's "Zack Snyder's Justice

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:57.339
League," the re-edit released on HBO Max,
both do the trick.

00:10:57.339 --> 00:11:01.339
These two films tell essentially the same
story and are equally relevant to Batman's

00:11:01.339 --> 00:11:04.350
overall place in the pop cultural universe.

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:09.910
But if time isn't a factor, since the "Snyder
cut" is literally twice as long as Whedon's

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:14.769
two-hour "Justice League," we think even the
most adamant Snyder critics would probably

00:11:14.769 --> 00:11:16.759
admit his take is better.

00:11:16.759 --> 00:11:22.259
Whedon's film has its moments, but it ultimately
amounts to a bland exercise in pushing a handful

00:11:22.259 --> 00:11:25.519
of intellectual properties through the market-demanded
motions.

00:11:25.519 --> 00:11:32.290
Meanwhile, the Snyder cut is a maximalist
cataclysm of R-rated action-fantasy lunacy.

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:36.740
2017's "Justice League" is a corporate product;
it is not art.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:41.850
The Snyder cut is also a corporate product
... but maybe it is art?

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:47.389
Either way, one thing is absolutely certain:
there's definitely more Batman stuff in the

00:11:47.389 --> 00:11:52.180
Snyder cut, just as there is more of literally
everything else.

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:57.639
"The Batman" opened to generally positive
feedback and appears as though it will more

00:11:57.639 --> 00:11:59.899
than likely produce a sequel or two.

00:11:59.899 --> 00:12:05.769
However, for right his second, it's a provisionally
standalone film whose identity within Batman's

00:12:05.769 --> 00:12:08.519
film oeuvre has yet to fully take shape.

00:12:08.519 --> 00:12:12.829
But even though it's the most recent movie
and thus logically the last one you should

00:12:12.829 --> 00:12:17.069
watch, there are actually a few different
options for placement of "The Batman" in your

00:12:17.069 --> 00:12:18.390
movie marathon.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:23.139
From the standpoint of its central character's
evolution, "The Batman" could fit before the

00:12:23.139 --> 00:12:24.879
Burton/Schumacher series.

00:12:24.879 --> 00:12:29.970
Though it's not an origin story in the traditional
sense, Robert Pattinson's depiction of Bruce

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.470
Wayne is a hero who's a little underbaked.

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:38.230
He only starts growing into a hyper-competent
crime smasher resembling the guy from 1989's

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:40.690
"Batman" at the movie's conclusion.

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:45.750
Then again, since culture and world events
of the 2000s heavily inform Nolan's trilogy,

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:50.750
maybe it makes more sense to watch the movies
in their real-world release order, as "The

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:55.829
Batman" director Matt Reeves infuses sentiments
of the 2010s and early 2020s into his Gotham

00:12:55.829 --> 00:12:56.970
City saga.

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:02.820
We do not recommend watching "The Batman"
immediately before or after the 1966 "Batman"

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:07.629
because the tonal clash between Paul Dano
and Frank Gorshin's interpretations of the

00:13:07.629 --> 00:13:12.180
Riddler may cause the viewer some internal
whiplash or psychic damage.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:17.139
Gorshin's Riddler never successfully kills
anybody, while Dano's Riddler kills a whole

00:13:17.139 --> 00:13:20.600
score of folks in nasty ways.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.420
No word though on how Tommy Lee Jones feels
about either of their performances.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:27.050
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:29.930
Plus, even more Looper videos about Batman
are coming soon.

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:35.000
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

